Description: CVE-2026-63385 - Reject HTTP headers containing CRLF (obs-fold header injection)
 evhttp_header_is_valid_value() previously accepted header values containing
 CR/LF sequences as long as they were followed by a space or tab, treating
 them as RFC obs-fold line folding. Since obs-fold is deprecated (RFC 9112)
 and certain proxy chains re-interpret such values, this allowed HTTP header
 injection. Reject any header value that contains a CR or LF character.
 .
 This addresses the CRLF / obs-fold header-injection half of
 GHSA-jcwh-pvf2-73p2. The regression test is adapted to the Debian 11
 (2.1.12-stable) test tree, which lacks the upstream empty-key assertion line.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/libevent/libevent/commit/758be0c0f69c1934ef9a84ab39e9f9e5fde2e6d0
Author: Nick Mathewson <nickm@torproject.org>
Last-Update: 2026-08-22
---
--- a/http.c
+++ b/http.c
@@ -1955,13 +1955,11 @@ evhttp_header_is_valid_value(const char *value)
 {
 	const char *p = value;

-	while ((p = strpbrk(p, "\r\n")) != NULL) {
-		/* we really expect only one new line */
-		p += strspn(p, "\r\n");
-		/* we expect a space or tab for continuation */
-		if (*p != ' ' && *p != '\t')
-			return (0);
+	if (strpbrk(p, "\r\n") != NULL) {
+		/* Reject any header containing CR or LF. */
+		return (0);
 	}
+
 	return (1);
 }

--- a/test/regress_http.c
+++ b/test/regress_http.c
@@ -2406,7 +2406,8 @@ http_bad_header_test(void *ptr)
 	TAILQ_INIT(&headers);

 	tt_want(evhttp_add_header(&headers, "One", "Two") == 0);
-	tt_want(evhttp_add_header(&headers, "One", "Two\r\n Three") == 0);
+	tt_want(evhttp_add_header(&headers, "One", "Two Three") == 0);
+	tt_want(evhttp_add_header(&headers, "One", "Two\r\n Three") == -1);
 	tt_want(evhttp_add_header(&headers, "One\r", "Two") == -1);
 	tt_want(evhttp_add_header(&headers, "One\n", "Two") == -1);
 	tt_want(evhttp_add_header(&headers, "One", "Two\r") == -1);
Description: CVE-2026-63388 - Fix heap out-of-bounds write via AF_UNIX+http+NDEBUG
 When running an evhttp server over an AF_UNIX socket, on a copy of libevent
 compiled with NDEBUG (the default for release builds), a crafted AF_UNIX peer
 address could cause a heap out-of-bounds write when recording the connection
 address in bufferevent_socket_set_conn_address_(). The address (up to ~110
 bytes for a sockaddr_un) was memcpy'd into a fixed-size union (~28 bytes,
 sockaddr_in6/sockaddr_in), guarded only by an EVUTIL_ASSERT that is compiled
 out under NDEBUG. Without NDEBUG the same condition aborts via the assertion.
 .
 The fix:
  - checks the provided address length unconditionally (regardless of NDEBUG);
  - returns EVUTIL_EAI_FAIL and handles the failure at both call sites (the
    evdns getaddrinfo callback and the evhttp accept path) instead of writing
    out of bounds;
  - enlarges conn_address to a struct sockaddr_storage so a full AF_UNIX
    address can be stored.
 .
 Resolves GHSA-cvq5-vrvr-j338 (CWE-787).
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/libevent/libevent/commit/52057cb33d0c20c0a0453fbabe6c0c96854931b9
Author: Nick Mathewson <nickm@torproject.org>
Last-Update: 2026-08-22
